                    "line": 324,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vishnunithyasoundhar S",
                        "email": "vishnunithyasoundhar.s@intel.com",
                        "username": "VishnuNithyaSoundharS"
                    },
                    "message": "the response tid may not correspond to our preallocated tid always.\n\nIf we change the preallocated tid, we will have to change it for all devices. \n\nHence preallocated TID was forced for all devices.\n\nBut I agree the logic should stay for mctp demux side\n\nSince for demux response tid will always match since they do a tid = eid mapping. \n\nI will put a #IFDEF so that this logic wont be used for af-mctp pldm"
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 324,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vishnunithyasoundhar S",
                        "email": "vishnunithyasoundhar.s@intel.com",
                        "username": "VishnuNithyaSoundharS"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @manojkiran.eda@gmail.com\n\nCould you please confirm whether this logic is required for PLDM when using mctp-dmux, or if it can be safely removed?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 324,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vishnunithyasoundhar S",
                        "email": "vishnunithyasoundhar.s@intel.com",
                        "username": "VishnuNithyaSoundharS"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Konstantin Aladyshev",
                        "email": "aladyshev22@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Kostr"
                    },
                    "message": "1) Is there a guarantee that PLDM support is the same between the different mediums?\n\n2) This would prioritize mediums that are available at stand-by over others. For example for a plug-n-play PCIe card this could prioritize slow I2C medium over fast PCIe VDM."
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arun P M",
                        "email": "arun.p.m@intel.com",
                        "username": "arun-pm"
                    },
                    "message": ">1) Is there a guarantee that PLDM support is the same between the different mediums?\n\nGood question. It is possible that a device can choose to provide different level of PLDM support over different bindings. \n- Such a device will have single TL PDR or multiple? \n- Also, any thoughts on how TID assignment will happen in such cases? \n\n\n> 2) This would prioritize mediums that are available at stand-by over others. For example for a plug-n-play PCIe card this could prioritize slow I2C medium over fast PCIe VDM.\n\nYou are right. This is something which we are also interested to implement.\n \nPLDM should choose available higher\u2011bandwidth MCTP binding mediums/network to communicate \u2014for example, PCIe would rank above I2C/I3C as you mentioned.\n\nAnother point is that both the mediums might not be available all the time. For instance, an I2C path to a PCIe card may be available earlier, while the PCIe path becomes usable only after PCIe enumeration completes. In such cases, PLDM could begin communication over I2C and then switch to PCIe once it becomes available.\n\n\n@jk@ozlabs.org Is it feasible to expose MCTP binding information under each MCTP device object paths to implement something like above? If not binding information, another option I can think of is the max speed/bandwidth of the network/binding."
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "> @jk@ozlabs.org Is it feasible to expose MCTP binding information under each MCTP device object paths to implement something like above?\n\nYes, but it's not as simple as you're proposing:\n\n - an EID-referenced endpoint object may have *no* physical binding associated (eg., where it is bridged)\n - an EID-referenced endpoint object may have *multiple* physical bindings associated (eg., where there are multiple paths to the endpoint, like DSP0236 Figure 9(A) & Figure 14)\n\nThere seem to be two use-cases above:\n\n 1. Where the endpoint's MCTP interfaces are the same across two transport types, and the transports should be selected based on some desired performance metric\n \n 2. Where the endpoint's MCTP interfaces vary across the two transports, so the transport must be selected on some application-level logic\n\nWhich influences the routing & EID allocation setup:\n \nFor (2), we would require separate EIDs to be assigned across those two transports, so that the application logic has some chance of determining which remote interface to interact with.\n \nIn this case, you would need some sort of policy to select an EID in the application layer, right? Or are you proposing we rely on mctpd's dbus objects to determine this (ie, using the proposed transport type data, noting that won't work for bridged cases)\n\nFor (1), I would suggest using the same EID across both transports on the remote; the selection of the \"best\" interface would then be a kernel routing decision, with no application awareness required.\n\nIn this case, nothing needs to consume the transport type data, it's only used at routing setup time."
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Arun P M",
                        "email": "arun.p.m@intel.com",
                        "username": "arun-pm"
                    },
                    "message": "> 1. Where the endpoint's MCTP interfaces are the same across two transport types, and the transports should be selected based on some desired performance metric\n\nI fully didn't understand this. I though mctpd always adds separate interfaces to D-Bus for each transport/port medium. Is that not the case?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "platform-mc/terminus_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vishnunithyasoundhar S",
                        "email": "vishnunithyasoundhar.s@intel.com",
                        "username": "VishnuNithyaSoundharS"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
